        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score points by capturing specific cards in tricks. Points: Nines (9 each), Fives (5 each), Aces (4 each), Kings (3), Queens (2), Jacks (1).',
          gameplay: '1. **Part One**: Play 13 tricks with the first deal. Trump is set by the first card led. 2. **Part Two**: Deal the remaining 26 cards (13 each). Play the remaining 13 tricks using the same trump suit. 3. **The Play**: Must follow suit if possible. If void, may trump or discard.',
          keyRules: [
            "The Five of Trumps is known as the 'Don'.",
            'Winning the last trick of the game (the 26th trick) scores no bonus points, but captures the cards.',
            'Players must follow suit if they can; trumps rank high.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Prioritize capturing the Nines. Since a single Nine is worth 9 points (more than an Ace and King combined), it is the most efficient way to build a lead.',
          intermediate: "The 'Part Two' Advantage: Use the first 13 tricks to identify which high cards your opponent is holding. In the second half, you will know exactly which suits they are void in.",
          advanced: "Trump Control: Save your 'Don' (5 of trumps) to capture an opponent's high-point Nine when they are forced to lead it or play it on a side-suit void.",
          tips: [
            'Never lead a Nine unless you are certain it will win the trick.',
            'Count the trumps carefully; there are 13 in total across both phases.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
