          deckDescription: 'One standard 52-card deck with four French suits (spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs), each containing thirteen ranks from ace down to two. No jokers are used; this deck supports Blind Don, a Lancashire pub point-trick game in which nines, fives, aces, kings, queens and jacks carry scoring values.',
          suitDescription: 'Four French suits (spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs). Trump is chosen by the first card led to the opening trick (the pitch), and players must follow suit whenever able. Point values are assigned to specific ranks (nine nine points, five five points, ace four, king three, queen two, jack one); other ranks score zero.',
          rankDescription: 'Thirteen ranks per suit; point values: nine counts nine, five counts five, ace four, king three, queen two, jack one, others zero. Trick order is standard; the goal is to reach a target score through taking point cards in tricks.',
          dealPattern: 'Thirteen cards to each player in two separate deals.',
          bettingLimits: 'None',
          actions: {},
          handRankingSystem: 'None; card point values (9=9, 5=5, A=4...) determine victory in the deal.',
          rankingSystem: 'Point-Trick (All Fours type)',
          useTrump: true,
          notApplicableReasons: {
            drawConfig: 'Cards are received in two fixed dealing rounds; no draw pile exists during the trick play.',
            discardConfig: 'Cards are played directly to tricks; no separate waste or discard pile is utilized.',
            specialCards: 'Nines and Fives have high point values but do not trigger automated secondary engine actions.',
            declarationMechanism: 'The trump is set by the physical card led; no hidden tokens or declaration phase exists.',
            fishingConfig: 'Blind Don is a trick-taking game and does not use a capture-from-tableau fishing mechanic.',
            meldConfig: 'Points are earned strictly through trick capture; no melding of card sets is part of the gameplay.',
            shedding: 'Blind Don is a trick-taking game where players maintain their hand throughout the hand; the goal is winning tricks, not emptying the hand.',
          },
